WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
The first film stopped new G-protein activation with a mark and its reader.

2
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.460
What ends the active signal already downstream?

3
00:00:12.183 --> 00:00:15.723
Each layer is an active pool with an input and an exit.

4
00:00:17.123 --> 00:00:19.303
Receptors produce active G proteins.

5
00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:23.603
Active G proteins help produce second messenger.

6
00:00:25.383 --> 00:00:28.843
Messenger activates a kinase, which adds phosphate marks to targets.

7
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:31.877
These pools overlap in time.

8
00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:39.217
Stopping new receptor output does not instantly erase GTP, messenger, or target

9
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:40.597
phosphate already present.

10
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:46.257
Each pool keeps changing until its own input and exit settle the balance.

11
00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:52.247
G-alpha carries GTP while active and hydrolyzes it to GDP.

12
00:00:53.687 --> 00:00:58.407
An RGS protein speeds that reaction for compatible G proteins.

13
00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:03.827
Faster hydrolysis shortens the lifetime of the active green pool.

14
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:10.623
A phosphodiesterase drains a cyclic-nucleotide pool by opening the messenger's

15
00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:15.803
phosphate ring. When synthesis and breakdown match, the pool holds steady.

16
00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:21.503
Slower breakdown makes the same input build a higher, longer-lasting pool.

17
00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:26.167
Now shut messenger synthesis off, then inhibit the drain.

18
00:01:27.527 --> 00:01:30.687
Can protecting the remaining messenger create a new signal?

19
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:40.273
No. No new messenger is made.

20
00:01:41.453 --> 00:01:43.073
An empty pool stays empty.

21
00:01:44.473 --> 00:01:48.333
Messenger already present is protected and decays more slowly.

22
00:01:49.573 --> 00:01:53.513
With continuing input, inhibition raises and prolongs the pool.

23
00:01:54.567 --> 00:01:58.007
The last pool here is phosphorylated target protein.

24
00:01:59.387 --> 00:02:01.447
A kinase adds each gold phosphate badge.

25
00:02:02.687 --> 00:02:04.747
A protein phosphatase removes it.

26
00:02:05.867 --> 00:02:10.467
The phosphorylated pool fades when removal overtakes new phosphorylation.

27
00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:15.687
If that phosphate activates the target, its activity fades too.

28
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.980
Changing an exit changes both amplitude and timing.

29
00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.320
A narrow exit allows an active pool to accumulate.

30
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:30.440
A wide exit keeps its level lower and clears it sooner after production stops.

31
00:02:31.533 --> 00:02:33.433
The same bookkeeping shapes time.

32
00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:38.373
Under constant input, an active pool rises toward a balance.

33
00:02:39.613 --> 00:02:42.113
When input stops, removal makes it decay.

34
00:02:43.373 --> 00:02:47.813
A faster removal process lowers the balance and clears the pool sooner.

35
00:02:48.917 --> 00:02:53.917
Some systems also build a slower adaptive brake while stimulation continues.

36
00:02:55.197 --> 00:02:56.897
A gap can let that brake reverse.

37
00:02:58.437 --> 00:03:03.657
Separated pulses offer recovery intervals that one steady input never provides.

38
00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:10.310
Separated pulses can preserve responsiveness because each gap allows recovery.

39
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.950
Constant input gives no such interval.

40
00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:20.290
Pituitary responses to gonadotropin-releasing hormone show this contrast.

41
00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:26.770
In gonadotrope cells, the main adaptation appears to lie downstream of the receptor.

42
00:03:27.983 --> 00:03:31.303
Receptor uncoupling is therefore only one timer.

43
00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:38.043
GTP hydrolysis, messenger breakdown, and dephosphorylation set later timers.

44
00:03:39.523 --> 00:03:44.623
Each downstream layer fades when removal outruns production, and the slowest

45
00:03:44.623 --> 00:03:47.963
surviving layer can outlast the receptor's activity.
